7203320118096068164\n12730499 353 18342458184340802896\n13402501 40 18408888438416943650\n13533116 47 17704073966988120734\n13668630 136 13045936893580447032\n13685833 64 18410577279417890355\n13914758 101 17274829009104040745\n14251764 18 17275386499410262665\n14294032 229 16444744931613138069\n14341114 176 18342178877404841240\n14386348 63 18335704939886834670\n14617045 38 18186520986190794107\n14856354 85 15213028155099959259\n14933364 13 18412545409969818385\n15183329 4 18271811193962741547\n15475509 35 17750216047255221514\n15537594 2 16950276317477908107\n17844677 252 18272658934592080752\n1813 80 17240762899858425620\n21033648 29 18130208424638183393\n21033650 10 16661493870424864780\n21267235 1 17967541199069355163\n21623969 137 18130793356123449110\n221357 26 18272650130236087216\n2215653 11 18410852153583683815\n22224240 67 18272081747370309482\n2297311 6 18114185220253653173\n23035841 295 17894630340035187715\n23559900 14 18337387249134503089\n239999 70 18408324393742428706\n3004659 81 17458350749031231476\n335352 9 18113899369464702501\n3383291 50 17750521840178974395\n34797466 226 17603591837630991604\n3545911 37 18334575737709076691\n4073 2 18261396701650787658\n4325135 7 17821731658926721044\n465052 167 18272374157502807974\n5104073 3 18271802368569865512\n5283156 175 16081080459618640384\n542803 24 15769779065108850034\n59755656 215 18333732386199509027\n\n> <PUBCHEM_SHAPE_MULTIPOLES>\n465.21\n20.25\n1.9\n0.96\n10.27\n0.36\n0.03\n0.97\n0.99\n-1.67\n-0.01\n-0.22\n-0.03\n2.1\n\n> <PUBCHEM_SHAPE_SELFOVERLAP>\n990.212\n\n> <PUBCHEM_SHAPE_VOLUME>\n263.2\n\n> <PUBCHEM_COORDINATE_TYPE>\n2\n5\n10\n\n$$$$\n","conformer":{"source":"PubChem 3D conformer","url":"https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/compound/4594#section=3D-Conformer"},"properties":{"weight":345.4,"xlogp":2.2,"hbd":1,"hba":6,"tpsa":96.3},"brandList":[{"name":"Losec","url":"https://medikamente.basg.gv.at/documents/1-19207__DOTC_FACH_INFO.pdf"}],"product":{"label":"Losec 20 mg gastro-resistant capsules","url":"https://medikamente.basg.gv.at/documents/1-19207__DOTC_FACH_INFO.pdf","source":"BASG"},"groups":[{"ruleId":"benzimidazole","name":"Benzimidazole","atoms":[4,5,10,11,13,16,18,19,20],"ring":[10,4,11,13,5],"count":1,"note":{"caption":"Protonating this ring is the second step that switches on activation.","body":["The benzimidazole N3 is much less basic than the pyridine nitrogen, so it only picks up a proton in strong acid. Once charged, it pulls electron density away from C2 and leaves that carbon open to attack by the pyridine nitrogen.","That attack begins the rearrangement to the reactive form, and the pH dependence of activation follows protonation of this ring."],"sources":[{"citation":"Roche VF. The chemically elegant proton pump inhibitors. Am J Pharm Educ 2006;70(5):101","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1637016/","licence":"Free full text"},{"citation":"Shin JM, Kim N. Pharmacokinetics and pharmacodynamics of the proton pump inhibitors. J Neurogastroenterol Motil 2013;19(1):25-35","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3548122/","licence":"CC BY-NC 3.0"}],"review":{"status":"unreviewed"}}},{"ruleId":"pyridine","name":"Pyridine ring","atoms":[6,8,9,12,14,17],"ring":[6,8,9,12,14,17],"count":1,"note":{"caption":"A weak base (pKa about 4) that traps the drug where acid is strongest.","body":["With a pKa near 4, the pyridine nitrogen is mostly protonated only in a very acidic space such as the secretory canaliculus of the parietal cell. The charged form cannot easily leave, so the drug builds up next to the H⁺/K⁺-ATPase, around 1000 times its blood level.","In its uncharged form the same nitrogen is the nucleophile that attacks the benzimidazole to start activation."],"sources":[{"citation":"Shin JM, Kim N. Pharmacokinetics and pharmacodynamics of the proton pump inhibitors. J Neurogastroenterol Motil 2013;19(1):25-35","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3548122/","licence":"CC BY-NC 3.0"},{"citation":"Roche VF. The chemically elegant proton pump inhibitors. Am J Pharm Educ 2006;70(5):101","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1637016/","licence":"Free full text"}],"review":{"status":"unreviewed"}}},{"ruleId":"sulfoxide","name":"Sulfoxide","atoms":[0,2,7,10],"ring":[],"count":1,"term":"prodrug","note":{"caption":"Inert as given; acid turns it into a sulfenamide that grabs pump cysteines.","body":["The sulfinyl bridge cannot bond to the enzyme as it stands. After two protonations, a rearrangement through a spiro intermediate gives a sulfenic acid and then a cyclic cationic sulfenamide, which forms disulfide bonds with cysteines of the H⁺/K⁺-ATPase (Cys813 and Cys892 for omeprazole).","The sulfur is a stereocentre, and omeprazole is a racemate of R and S forms. Calling it a prodrug is debated, as activation needs acid, not enzymes."],"sources":[{"citation":"Roche VF. The chemically elegant proton pump inhibitors. Am J Pharm Educ 2006;70(5):101","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1637016/","licence":"Free full text"},{"citation":"Shin JM, Kim N. Pharmacokinetics and pharmacodynamics of the proton pump inhibitors. J Neurogastroenterol Motil 2013;19(1):25-35","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3548122/","licence":"CC BY-NC 3.0"},{"citation":"ChEBI. omeprazole (CHEBI:7772)","url":"https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/searchId.do?chebiId=CHEBI:7772","licence":"CC BY 4.0"}],"review":{"status":"unreviewed"}}},{"ruleId":"aryl_ether","name":"Aryl ether","atoms":[1,3,12,19,22,23],"ring":[],"count":2,"note":{"caption":"Methoxy groups tune the electron density of both rings.","body":["Omeprazole carries two methoxy groups: one at position 4 of the pyridine and one at position 5 of the benzimidazole. The pyridine methoxy donates electrons by resonance to the ring nitrogen, making the uncharged nitrogen a better nucleophile for the activation step.","Pantoprazole swaps the benzimidazole methoxy for a difluoromethoxy, and that change lowers its reactivity."],"sources":[{"citation":"Roche VF. The chemically elegant proton pump inhibitors. Am J Pharm Educ 2006;70(5):101","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1637016/","licence":"Free full text"},{"citation":"ChEBI. omeprazole (CHEBI:7772)","url":"https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/searchId.do?chebiId=CHEBI:7772","licence":"CC BY 4.0"}],"review":{"status":"unreviewed"}}}],"pubchemUrl":"https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/compound/4594"}